## Why This Is Asked
This angle tests the ability to quantify a data-heavy feature with a clear offline-first constraint, balancing device resources against business value.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first architectures
- Local indexing and delta synchronization
- Resource budgeting (CPU, memory, storage)
- Data transfer costs and break-even analysis

## Code Example
```
// Implementation sketch: simple local search index
class LocalIndex {
  constructor() { this.docs = []; }
  build(docs) { this.docs = docs.map(d => ({ id: d.id, text: d.content })); }
  search(q) {
    const terms = q.toLowerCase().split(/\s+/);
    return this.docs.filter(d => terms.every(t => d.text.toLowerCase().includes(t)));
  }
  deltaUpdate(changes) {
    // apply lightweight patches to docs
    changes.forEach(c => {
      const idx = this.docs.findIndex(d => d.id === c.id);
      if (idx >= 0) this.docs[idx] = { ...this.docs[idx], ...c.patch };
      else this.docs.push({ id: c.id, text: c.patch.content });
    });
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement delta-sync to minimize data transfer while ensuring consistency?
- What privacy controls would you layer to protect sensitive docs during offline usage?

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses a candidate's ability to translate product goals into a financially grounded technical plan, balancing on-device ML costs, user privacy, and rollout risk.

## Key Concepts

- On-device ML cost modeling (energy, memory, inference latency)
- Data transfer savings and network impact
- Privacy controls and regulatory compliance
- OTA update cadence and maintenance costs
- Rollout strategy, drift monitoring, and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-cost model for CBA calculations
function cba(params) {
  const energy = params.inferences * params.energyPerInference;
  const memory = params.modelSize + params.cacheSize;
  const dataSavings = params.uploadSize * params.reductionRate;
  return { energy, memory, dataSavings };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a practical, new angle on CBA: on-device prefetching to cut latency, balancing storage, energy, privacy, and business impact. Tests the ability to translate UX improvements into measurable costs and decision criteria.

## Key Concepts

- On-device predictor energy/compute
- Cache budgeting and invalidation strategies
- Data transfer vs. storage costs
- Privacy/opt-in and data minimization
- Latency impact and engagement metrics
- Break-even analysis and go/no-go thresholds

## Code Example

```javascript
function calcBreakeven(params){
  const {users, cacheMB, energyPerHit, savedMsPerHit, valuePerMs, pricePerMB} = params;
  const energyCost = energyPerHit * users;
  const dataValue = savedMsPerHit * users * valuePerMs;
  const breakevenMonths = (cacheMB * pricePerMB) / ((dataValue) - energyCost);
  return breakevenMonths;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you calibrate the model without A/B tests for small pilots?
- What privacy constraints would you enforce for on-device prefetching data?

